\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A graph $G=(V,E)$ is finite, simple and undirected. It is connected if every two vertices are joined by a path. It is vertex-transitive if for every $u,v\in V$ there exists a bijection $\phi:V\to V$ preserving adjacency with $\phi(u)=v$. A Hamiltonian path is an ordering $v_1,\ldots,v_n$ of all $n=|V|$ vertices such that $v_iv_{i+1}\in E$ for $1\le i<n$. A single vertex is itself a Hamiltonian path.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Does every nonempty finite connected vertex-transitive graph admit a Hamiltonian path? Explicitly, can one order all its vertices as $v_1,\ldots,v_n$ with every consecutive pair adjacent? The path need not close up to a cycle. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.04618v2}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can every vertex of a finite connected graph having the same symmetry type at every vertex be visited exactly once along one path?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 1329 (GRAPH-005). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Carla Groenland, Sean Longbrake, Raphael Steiner, J\'er\'emie Turcotte and Liana Yepremyan, \emph{Longest cycles in vertex-transitive and highly connected graphs} (2025), arXiv:2408.04618v2, Section 1, p. 2, formulation of Lov\'asz's 1969 conjecture. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.04618v2}.
